dbdb is proprietary software. Its source code is not published. Its use
is free for individuals and for smaller organisations. Larger organisations
need a paid commercial licence. The terms are below.


1. Definitions

"The Software" means the dbdb programs for the desktop, the command line
and the browser as the Licensor provides them, with their documentation
and examples.

"You" means the person or the organisation using the Software. An
organisation is counted together with every organisation that controls it,
is controlled by it, or is under common control with it.

"Large Organisation" means an organisation of any kind, public bodies
included, that in its last completed financial year had

  (a) more than 10 people working for it, employees and contractors
      together, or
  (b) a revenue, or for a body without revenue a budget, of more than
      5 million Swiss francs or the same amount in another currency.

"Commercial Licence" means a separate written agreement with the Licensor.


2. Free use

You may use the Software free of charge, for any purpose including
commercial work, if

  (a) You are an individual using it for yourself, or
  (b) You are an organisation that is not a Large Organisation.

A Large Organisation may use the Software free of charge to evaluate it,
for no more than 30 days in total.

Use on behalf of someone else counts as use by them. A consultant or
contractor who uses the Software in work for a Large Organisation needs
that organisation to hold a Commercial Licence, whatever the consultant's
own size.


3. Use that needs a Commercial Licence

Any use by or on behalf of a Large Organisation beyond the evaluation in
section 2 needs a Commercial Licence. The README says how to get one.


4. What You may do

Within the use that sections 2 and 3 allow, You may

  (a) run the Software on any number of machines You control, and
  (b) make the copies that running it and keeping backups need.


5. What You may not do

Without the Licensor's written permission You may not

  (a) give, sell, rent or sublicense the Software to anyone else,
  (b) offer the Software to others as a hosted or managed service,
  (c) decompile, disassemble or otherwise reverse engineer the Software,
      or change it or build other software from it, except where the law
      gives You that right and it cannot be excluded,
  (d) remove or change licence or copyright notices, or
  (e) get around any technical means that tell free use from licensed use.

This licence gives You no right to the source code. Source code that
reaches You in any way stays the Licensor's and is covered by these same
limits.


6. What You make with it stays Yours

This licence makes no claim on Your data, on the projects, jobs, queries
and other files You make with the Software, or on what they produce.


7. Parts by others

The Software contains components by other authors under their own
licences, among them the Terminus TTF font under the SIL Open Font License
1.1. Those licences apply to those components in place of this one.


8. End of the licence

Your rights under this licence end if You break its terms and have not put
that right within 30 days of the Licensor telling You so. They also end, as
far as free use goes, when You become a Large Organisation; You then have
90 days to obtain a Commercial Licence or stop using the Software. When
Your rights end, You must stop using the Software and delete Your copies.


9. No warranty, limited liability

The Software is provided as it is, without warranty of any kind, express or
implied, including fitness for a particular purpose. You are responsible
for checking what it does to Your data. As far as the law allows, the
Licensor is not liable for any damage that comes from using the Software or
from not being able to use it. Liability that the law does not allow to be
excluded, such as for intent or gross negligence, is not affected.


10. Law and courts

This licence is governed by Swiss law, without its rules on conflict of
laws. The courts at the Licensor's place of residence have exclusive
jurisdiction.


11. Other versions

The Licensor may publish new versions of this licence. A release of the
Software stays under the version of the licence it was published with.
